203125, 0.0207366943359375, 0.043487548828125, -0.0281219482421875, 0.005527496337890625, 0.0246124267578125, 0.0275115966796875, -0.006328582763671875, -0.04388427734375, 0.003574371337890625, 0.03582763671875, -0.027862548828125, -0.0017547607421875, 0.030853271484375, 0.004985809326171875, 0.0186309814453125, -0.01641845703125, -0.0328369140625, -0.0050048828125, 0.0227203369140625, 0.01093292236328125, 0.0010423660278320312, 0.0207977294921875, 0.017822265625, 0.0271148681640625, -0.0294342041015625, 0.033721923828125, -0.01494598388671875, -0.0059814453125, 0.0110626220703125, -0.04443359375, 0.016632080078125, 0.023101806640625, 0.0296478271484375, -0.003993988037109375, 0.0010995864868164062, -0.01181793212890625, 0.02728271484375, -0.00212860107421875, -0.007904052734375, 0.02496337890625, -0.0229949951171875, -0.010589599609375, -0.015869140625, -0.025665283203125, 0.03814697265625, 0.0302734375, 0.00843048095703125, 0.00433349609375, -0.019500732421875, 0.0028095245361328125, -0.00560760498046875, -0.0009455680847167969, 0.007160186767578125, 0.00873565673828125, -0.002819061279296875, 0.0282135009765625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Antibiotics and other antimicrobial agents are essential tools in modern medicine for combating infectious diseases. This overview is intended for students and general readers seeking to understand the distinctions, mechanisms, and limitations of these agents, as well as the challenges posed by antibiotic resistance.\n\n**Antibiotics vs. Other Antimicrobials**\n\nAntimicrobials are a broad category of agents that act against various microorganisms, including bacteria, viruses, fungi, and protozoa. Antibiotics are a subset of antimicrobials specifically active against bacteria. Other classes include antivirals (targeting viruses), antifungals (targeting fungi), and antiprotozoals (targeting protozoa). For example, penicillin is an antibiotic, acyclovir is an antiviral, fluconazole is an antifungal, and metronidazole can act as both an antibiotic and an antiprotozoal. Some agents, like metronidazole, blur these categories by exhibiting activity against more than one type of microorganism. In such cases, classification is typically based on the agent\u2019s primary clinical use or its main spectrum of activity.\n\n**Mechanisms of Action**\n\nAntibiotics work through two main mechanisms: bactericidal antibiotics kill bacteria directly (e.g., penicillin disrupts bacterial cell wall synthesis), while bacteriostatic antibiotics inhibit bacterial growth (e.g., tetracycline interferes with protein synthesis). Other antimicrobials have distinct mechanisms: antivirals may inhibit viral replication or entry into host cells (e.g., acyclovir inhibits viral DNA polymerase), antifungals often target fungal cell membrane synthesis (e.g., fluconazole inhibits ergosterol synthesis), and antiprotozoals may disrupt DNA synthesis or metabolic pathways in protozoa.\n\n**Limitations and Exceptions**\n\nAntibiotics are generally ineffective against viruses and most fungi because these organisms lack the bacterial structures that antibiotics target, such as peptidoglycan cell walls or bacterial ribosomes. For example, penicillins and tetracyclines do not affect viruses or fungi. However, some antibiotics, like metronidazole, also act against certain protozoa by disrupting DNA synthesis, illustrating that classification can be ambiguous when agents have broader effects.\n\n**Antibiotic Resistance**\n\nA major concern with antibiotic use is the development of resistance. Bacteria can become resistant through mechanisms such as producing enzymes that inactivate antibiotics (e.g., beta-lactamase production), altering drug targets, or using efflux pumps to expel antibiotics. Overuse and misuse of antibiotics in medicine and agriculture accelerate the spread of resistant strains, leading to harder-to-treat infections. Globally, antibiotic resistance is estimated to cause hundreds of thousands of deaths annually and has led to notable outbreaks of multidrug-resistant bacteria. Strategies to combat resistance include appropriate prescribing, completing prescribed courses, and infection control measures.\n\n**Summary Table: Antibiotics vs. Other Antimicrobials**\n\n| Agent Type     | Primary Target | Example         | Mechanism (Typical)                |\n|----------------|---------------|-----------------|------------------------------------|\n| Antibiotic     | Bacteria      | Penicillin      | Inhibits cell wall synthesis       |\n| Antiviral      | Viruses       | Acyclovir       | Inhibits viral DNA polymerase      |\n| Antifungal     | Fungi         | Fluconazole     | Inhibits ergosterol synthesis      |\n| Antiprotozoal  | Protozoa      | Metronidazole   | Disrupts DNA synthesis             |\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nAntibiotics are a vital subset of antimicrobials, primarily targeting bacteria, but some agents have broader effects. Understanding their distinctions, mechanisms, and limitations is essential for responsible use and for addressing the global challenge of antibiotic resistance."}}